course: dictionary
lesson: 7006
title: Words about worry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Paranoid is an adjective. It describes someone who is too sure that people are against them, talking about them or trying to trick them, even when there is no good reason. Look at the context: «When we are worried, the brain fills gaps with the worst story.» The clues are worried and worst story, so paranoid is about imagining the worst.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Someone who wrongly believes everyone is out to get them. Now swap your meaning into the sentence: "When we are worried, the brain fills gaps with the worst story; paranoid means wrongly sure people are against you." It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Everyday talk and health talk use the word in different ways. "Don't be paranoid" tells a friend they are worrying too much about something that is not happening. In health, paranoid describes an illness that needs a doctor and kindness, not teasing.
explain: Being careful is not the same as being paranoid. Checking that a website is safe before you type your password is careful. Believing a stranger is spying on you when there is no sign of it is paranoid.
fact: Paranoid means too worried that people are against you, without good reason.
fact: In health, «paranoid» describes an illness that makes a person have strong false beliefs that others want to harm them.
fact: A person with this illness needs a doctor and kind support, not teasing.
fact: Being careful online, such as keeping your password private, is wise; it is not the same as being paranoid.
fact: «Paranoid» is an adjective: more paranoid, most paranoid. The noun «a paranoid» is rare.
exercise: Mia thinks everyone in class is secretly plotting against her, though nobody has said or done anything. Which word fits best? | *Paranoid | Generous | Curious | Cheerful
exercise: Dad says, «Don't be paranoid, your friends are not talking about you.» What does Dad mean? | *You are worrying too much about something that is not happening | You are very hungry | You are being very careful with your money | You have a cold
exercise: Which one shows careful behaviour rather than paranoia? | Thinking the dog is spying on you | *Checking that a website is safe before you type your password | Believing a stranger is stealing your bike when there is no sign of it | Hiding from the postman
test: Which option is the best meaning of paranoid? | Grey and wet | Short and rude | *Too worried that people are against you, with no good reason | Very old
test: Which is the kindest response to a friend who has paranoid thoughts because of an illness? | Tease them until they stop | *Encourage them to talk to a doctor and offer kindness | Tell them to stop being silly and ignore it | Laugh at their fears
exam: Which sentence uses paranoid correctly? | Sam ran paranoid to the shop | *Sam was sure the whole class was laughing at him, though nobody was | The paranoid sun rose over the hill | Sam ate a paranoid apple for lunch
